+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more groups of a whole number followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for hours,
+`m` for minutes and `s` for seconds. Each unit may appear at most once and in that order, with no spaces, like `2d`,
+`1h30m`, `90m` or `1d2h3m4s`. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. Negative and decimal values are
+not accepted. A {class}`datetime.timedelta` passed as a default is used as is.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=30m1h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
